    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d206">

                <pb facs="0427" n="353" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg353"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to the President’s Special
                        Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d206-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-01-10">January 10, 1942</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I should be grateful if you would look at this. The statements marked by me in
                    red would, if true, be catastrophic.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d206-f2"/></p>
                <p>There surely should be a joint Consultative Board so that your people can hear
                    what we have to say before taking decisions on allocation.</p>
                <p>Perhaps you will let me know what you advise.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. C.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>10/1/42.</p>
                </postscript>




                <xi:include href="d206-a1.xml" xpointer="d206-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d206-a2.xml" xpointer="d206-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d206-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d206-f1-content">
                <p>The source text, marked “Copy”, bears Churchill’s typewritten initials.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d206-f2" n="2" xml:id="d206-f2-content">
                <p>The statements and figures marked in red by Churchill on the attached memorandum
                    (final paragraph of section 2) are indicated by underscoring.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
